  "title": "Enovate daily position report: circulated by email for approval",
  "task_class": "pnl_reporting",
  "label_mechanical": "P&L and position reporting requests: distribute position / daily / report",
  "narrative": {
    "what_happens": "A daily position report for the Enovate book (referred to in the mail as the \"Enovate DPR\") is compiled and sent out by email, usually as an attached workbook dated to a single flow day. The sender passes it on with a short covering line \u2014 \"For your approval\" \u2014 and recipients reply on the same thread to confirm or to flag it as a draft or preliminary version, so some flow days appear as a draft and then a confirmed pass. Requests for a different form of the file are handled by hand in the same thread, as in the reply supplying \"an unprotected copy\". The same distribution pattern appears once for a separate daily file, the \"EES MODEL\" report, which is forwarded onward rather than produced by the sender. The output is an emailed spreadsheet plus an emailed approval; no system of record for the sign-off is visible in these messages.",
    "where_it_stalls": "The report has no visible approval channel other than a reply on the mail thread, so the \"Prelim\"/\"Draft\" version and the approved version circulate as separate emails for the same date and the reader has to work out which one is current from the subject line. File-format handling is also manual: a protected workbook goes out and an unprotected copy is produced only after someone asks. None of the messages here carry rework, waiting or deadline language, so the friction visible in this cluster is distribution and versioning rather than chasing.",
    "why_it_recurs": "The report is dated to a single day, so a new instance is produced and re-approved for every flow date, with the same small set of recipients each time.",
    "automation_opportunity": "Publish the daily position file to a shared location on a schedule, with the flow date and a draft/final state stamped on the file itself, and record the approver's sign-off against that record so the mail thread stops being the version-control mechanism. Generating the recipient-appropriate (unprotected) form as part of the same scheduled step would remove the ad-hoc re-send.",
    "coherence": "mixed"
  },
